      "text": "I'm not able to retrieve or provide the full text of *Project Hail Mary* by Andy Weir. Here's why, and what you can do instead:\n\n**Why I can't help with this specific request:**\n*Project Hail Mary* (2021) is a **currently under copyright** commercial novel published by Ballantine Books. Reproducing or distributing the full text — regardless of the stated purpose — would constitute copyright infringement. The academic framing (stylometric analysis) doesn't create a legal or ethical exception for obtaining a full unauthorized copy of a copyrighted work.\n\n---\n\n**Legitimate ways to get a machine-readable copy for academic research:**\n\n1. **Your University Library**\n - Many university libraries license **ebook versions** (via OverDrive, ProQuest Ebook Central, or similar). You may be able to export text excerpts through those platforms for academic use.\n - Talk to your **subject librarian** — they often know about fair-use tools and text-mining licensing agreements your institution holds.\n\n2. **Publisher / Rights Request**\n - Contact **Penguin Random House** (the publisher) directly and explain your academic research purpose. Publishers sometimes grant limited text-mining licenses to researchers.\n\n3. **HathiTrust Research Center (HTRC)**\n - The HTRC offers a **non-consumptive research** model where you can run computational analyses (exactly like stylometry) on copyrighted texts *without* receiving the raw text — the analysis results come back to you, not the book itself. This is designed for precisely this kind of work.\n\n4. **Your Campus Bookstore / Kindle Version**\n - If you've already purchased the paperback, you could also buy the **Kindle edition** (~$13), which you legally own. Some stylometric tools can work with Kindle files, and there are legal personal-use format-shifting arguments for materials you own.\n\n5. **Work with Weir's Public Domain / Freely Licensed Work**\n - Andy Weir originally self-published *The Martian* online for free. While it's now also under commercial copyright, this history means some of his **shorter early works or interviews** may be more accessible for comparison.\n\n6. **Stylometric Tools Built for This**\n - Tools like **JGAAP**, **Stylo (R package)**, and **NLTK pipelines** are often used in academic stylometry. Some come with sample corpora and are designed to interface with library-licensed text sources.\n\n---\n\nYour professor may also be able to help — instructors who assign this kind of computational analysis often have arrangements with the library or publisher, or can point you to a corpus your department has already licensed. That's the right path forward here!",
